# Public validation and framing circuit draft

This component supplies explicit Boolean circuits for the fixed-shape mathematical public request boundary. It is separate from the Rust compiler, native execution, admission controller and complete IdealFrontierSim composition. It does not qualify B6 or production. Source and kernel files remain draft pending independent review.

`PublicShape` selects a classical family: row candidate/comparator and role REG/VOTE. REG has one body byte length in 0..4096 and six opaque byte lengths in 0..256; VOTE has an actual list count in 0..1024. The circuit receives exact bytes at these lengths, fixed-width public fields, iv_pre16 and salt32. This host family selection is not a charged coherent parser, length dispatcher or proof decoder. Rejection before the typed request boundary remains outside this component. The role enum and bounds in the constructor validate the host interface; they are not claimed as gates.

`vote_validation` implements P0 for every bit value at a fixed VOTE shape. It computes the source's first error, in this order: vote byte outside0/1/2; actual list count outside3..1000; first adjacent pair failing strict unsigned bytewise order; stated u16 count unequal to actual count; supplied list digest unequal to the recomputed concrete SHA3-512 digest. Equality, lexicographic order and the first-failure index are explicit bit operations. No sorting, deduplication, RAM lookup or unpriced equality predicate is used. The output contains valid1, a local3-bit error code, offending index16, actual count16, declared count16 and vote byte8. Only the selected variant's payload is nonzero. These finite mathematical fields represent the source error values; they are not a Rust enum memory layout or an on-wire codec.

P0 computes bounded concrete hash work even on a fixed-shape request whose earlier check rejects it; that overcomputation is counted. There is no F call in P0. Its input may already include iv_pre/salt, which this validation does not modify or inspect. The source verifier's earlier proof-length check and the external typed constructors remain separate controller obligations.

`prepare_public` implements P1 conditional on an accepted typed request. It computes the row profile, both namespaces, body/authorization hashes and the exact relation public bytes. REG derives its body digest from body bytes rather than accepting B. VOTE receives B/scope/policy_hash as actual typed public fields; their preimages are not part of the proof interface and are not invented inputs here. All frame headers are explicit u16/u32 little-endian constant wires. Tags and fields match the guarded source order. The relation output is:

- REG: commitment block200, authorization block200, C128, auth64 bytes.
- VOTE: commitment block200, nullifier block200, authorization block200, list128n, nf128, auth64 bytes.

Each public block is exactly tag4 || P16 || zero48 || digest64 || 1f000080 || zero64. The zero48 is the placeholder at which the relation later inserts the secret; this component has no witness input. Separate outputs are mu64, iv16, namespace77, iv_pre16 and salt32.

The profile does not use ideal F. `table_bytes` reproduces `crt::codec::encode_words`: LEu64 magic/lambda/tau/grinding/multiplication words followed by TREE_MODULI (promoted from u16) and M_TREE/F/G/W_TREE/W_GATE/W_CRT. Candidate CRT_BYTES has84304 bytes; comparator has83704. Concrete SHAKE256 of these bytes gives T_crt64. Descriptor Frame, SHA3-512 D, and both SHA3-512 namespace digests are charged. Public constants receive real wires and X gates where set, rather than a free prehashed D or namespace.

`public_hash.py` uses the frozen concrete Keccak1600 clean permutation kernel. The separate lazy SHAKE256-64 wrapper has rate136 bytes, suffix1f and final rate bit; its SHA3-512 subclass has rate72, suffix06 and final rate bit. These wrappers retain every absorption state and use one shared clean permutation workspace. They avoid materializing every gate as a host object. Their gate streams remain finite and explicit.

For message length n, rate r, padding-bit count p (6 for SHAKE,3 for SHA3), and k=floor(n/r)+1, each clean hash kernel uses exactly 2k permutation invocations, X=2k*76972+2p, CX=2k*539372+32n+512+2p, CCX=2k*76800. Clean work is192086+1600+8n+p+1600k bits. Empty inputs use one untouched dummy source bit. No ideal-oracle call occurs. The candidate/comparator CRT sponge has620/616 blocks respectively.

The backend is one immutable F: bytes→264 bytes. H_mu frames namespace || F_REG/F_VOTE ||08, and H4 frames namespace || iv_pre ||mu ||04. A prefix kernel computes the full264-byte response into scratch, copies its64/16-byte prefix, then invokes the same F again to clean scratch. P1's outer compute/copy/uncompute invokes both prefix kernels again: exactly8 total F calls. Temporary canonical mu is retained for H4, so an arbitrary caller output-XOR target is never used as a hash input. Concrete public hashing is not silently replaced with F.

The compiler retains all SSA history, copies selected outputs by CNOT and reverses the complete computation. Its clean resource formula is2*forward_cost+one CNOT per output bit, with one maximum shared clean kernel workspace, plus retained history. Registers are disjoint and clean work starts zero. The joint map is |input,target,0>→|input,target XOR result(input),0>. Input labels are never targeted; correlations with output can entangle input and output for coherent inputs, so no unchanged reduced-state claim is made.

P1 defaults to accepted requests. `totalized_fixed_shape=True` explicitly exposes the same circuit on arbitrary content within a VOTE shape of3..1000 entries, including invalid vote bytes, list order, stated count and digest. That mode makes no standalone source rejection claim: an outer validity controller must mask every persistent write/result, expose the typed P0 error and undo all temporary work. F must remain immutable with no intervening reprogramming or interleaving. Internal F calls occur and remain charged; they are not asserted absent. Counts outside3..1000 still only use P0 and are a dispatcher obligation. In either mode, the invalid-request controller that masks persistent writes/results and cleans all temporary work is not yet composed. Nor is the finite family dispatcher. `complete_public_framing`, `complete_machine`, `complete_resource_vector`, `production_qualified`, cryptographic review and physical calibration flags remain false. This component's resource counts cannot be substituted for a complete sampler certificate.

The selected provenance guard binds13 Rust semantic files and the two generated CRT tables. `load_public_keccak` also binds the exact frozen Keccak helper and Boolean IR. This is selected source correspondence, not the full compiler/dependency closure; Python loader/runtime and immutable F interface remain premises. `profile_reference` and test hashlib use are independent expected-byte calculations, never counted circuit execution or a substitute for the emitted gates.

Validation is in progress. Tests distinguish independent expected frames/digests, actual concrete permutation evaluation, actual emitted clean gate streams, malformed interfaces and source drift. No Rust build, worker, device, physical-cost or cryptographic qualification is claimed.
